That makes sense if you treat “man” less as a fixed stereotype and more as a mature relational role.

A boy can know women mainly as objects of curiosity, desire, approval, or mystery. A more mature man can hold a fuller model:

her perspective
+ her incentives
+ her fears
+ her autonomy
+ her desires
+ his own desires
+ the consequences of how they interact

That added resolution changes how he moves.

So the transformation is not really:

know more about women → gain power over women

It is closer to:

know more about women → understand the relational field better → act with more judgment, restraint, confidence, and reciprocity

That is why empathy can feel masculine rather than contrary to masculinity. It gives you more degrees of freedom. You can read the room better, communicate better, protect without patronizing, flirt without fumbling, lead without flattening the other person, and distinguish what you want from what she wants.

I’d call it Relational Literacy.

Knowledge becomes transformational when it stops being trivia about the other sex and becomes a better model of another person. Then it feeds back into your own identity:

better model of her
→ better model of yourself in relation to her
→ better choices
→ more adult role

That is probably the counterintuitive part you are feeling: sometimes understanding the “other” more deeply makes you more fully yourself.